# Wallos scheduled jobs.
#
# Two things every line does, and both are about being able to tell that a job
# failed without being told by a user.
#
# WALLOS_CRON_STRICT=1 asks the job to put its outcome in its exit status. It
# is set here rather than defaulted in the code because startup.sh runs four of
# these same scripts under `set -e` before the container finishes starting, and
# an installation that cannot reach GitHub or whose rate provider refuses its
# key should still boot. A run nobody is watching is exactly the one that wants
# the status, and that is this file.
#
# The output is split rather than merged. Standard output is the job's running
# commentary and stays in a file — truncated by each run, because the file
# answers "what did the last run say" while history lives in the cron_runs
# table, and a file nothing rotates must not grow (#85). Standard error is
# where the reporting harness
# writes its one ERROR line, and /proc/1/fd/2 is the container's own stderr, so
# that line reaches `docker logs` instead of a file inside the container that
# nobody mounts and nothing rotates.
#
# A job that fails also records itself in the cron_runs table, which the admin
# page reads. That is the signal that survives a container being recreated, and
# the only one that shows a job which stopped being started at all.

# Run the scripts every day
0 1 * * * WALLOS_CRON_STRICT=1 /usr/local/bin/php /var/www/html/endpoints/cronjobs/updatenextpayment.php > /tmp/cron/updatenextpayment.log 2>> /proc/1/fd/2
0 2 * * * WALLOS_CRON_STRICT=1 /usr/local/bin/php /var/www/html/endpoints/cronjobs/updateexchange.php > /tmp/cron/updateexchange.log 2>> /proc/1/fd/2

